data.content:
Here's the xUnit equivalent:

```csharp
public class CustomerRepositoryTests : IClassFixture<CustomerRepositoryTests.DatabaseFixture>, IDisposable
{
    private readonly DbContext _context;
    private readonly CustomerRepository _repo;

    // [OneTimeSetUp] / [OneTimeTearDown] → IClassFixture + a fixture class
    public class DatabaseFixture : IDisposable
    {
        public DatabaseFixture()
        {
            Database.Initialize();       // OneTimeSetUp
        }

        public void Dispose()
        {
            Database.Cleanup();          // OneTimeTearDown
        }
    }

    // [SetUp] → constructor
    public CustomerRepositoryTests(DatabaseFixture _)
    {
        _context = new TestDbContext();
        _repo = new CustomerRepository(_context);
    }

    // [TearDown] → IDisposable.Dispose
    public void Dispose()
    {
        _context.Dispose();
    }

    [Fact]
    public void FindById_ExistingCustomer_ReturnsCustomer()
    {
        var customer = _repo.FindById(1);
        Assert.NotNull(customer);
        Assert.Equal("Alice", customer.Name);
    }
}
```

**Key mappings:**

| NUnit | xUnit |
|-------|-------|
| `[SetUp]` | Constructor |
| `[TearDown]` | `IDisposable.Dispose()` |
| `[OneTimeSetUp]` | `IClassFixture<T>` constructor |
| `[OneTimeTearDown]` | Fixture's `Dispose()` |
| `[Test]` | `[Fact]` |
| `Assert.That(x, Is.Not.Null)` | `Assert.NotNull(x)` |
| `Assert.That(x, Is.EqualTo(y))` | `Assert.Equal(y, x)` |

xUnit creates a new test class instance per test (like NUnit does with `[SetUp]`/`[TearDown]`), so the constructor/Dispose pattern is idiomatic. The `IClassFixture<T>` shared instance is injected via the constructor and lives for the entire test class.
